
# Installing React (Beginner-friendly)

This guide explains how to install and start a React project on macOS for complete beginners. It uses the simplest tools and shows the exact commands to run. If you are new to the command line, follow each step slowly — I can walk you through them interactively if you want.

## 1 — Open Terminal
- Press Command+Space, type Terminal, press Enter.

## 2 — Install Node.js (includes npm)
The easiest way is to use the official installer.
1. Open https://nodejs.org in your browser.
2. Download the LTS (Long Term Support) macOS installer (.pkg) and run it.
3. After installation, verify in Terminal:
```bash
node -v
npm -v
```
You should see version numbers for both commands.

If you prefer a package manager (advanced), you can use Homebrew — ask me if you'd like that alternative.

## 3 — Create a new React app (Create React App) — beginner route
1. In Terminal, run:
```bash
npx create-react-app my-app
cd my-app
npm start
```
2. `npm start` opens a development server at http://localhost:3000 and refreshes as you edit files.

Notes:
- `npx` runs the Create React App tool without installing it globally.
- Replace `my-app` with your preferred project folder name.

## 4 — Modern faster alternative (Vite)
If you want a snappier dev server and lighter setup, use Vite:
```bash
npm create vite@latest my-app -- --template react
cd my-app
npm install
npm run dev
```
This will start a dev server (usually at http://localhost:5173).

## 4b — Setting up React with TypeScript
If you'd like TypeScript (typed JavaScript) for better editor assistance and safer code, create a TypeScript React project.

Using Create React App (simplest for beginners):
```bash
npx create-react-app my-app --template typescript
cd my-app
npm start
```

Using Vite (faster start):
```bash
npm create vite@latest my-app -- --template react-ts
cd my-app
npm install
npm run dev
```

Notes:
- Files use `.tsx` for components that include JSX and `.ts` for plain TypeScript files.
- The project includes a `tsconfig.json` configured for React; you rarely need to edit it at first.
- If you add libraries that don't include types, install type definitions, for example:
```bash
npm install --save-dev @types/react @types/react-dom
```
- To check types manually, your editor (VS Code) will highlight type errors, or run `npm run build` which runs a type check in many setups.

## Interactive prompts and recommended choices (step-by-step)
If you prefer the simplest, most minimal TypeScript setup, I recommend using Vite. Below are exact commands and what to choose if the tool asks questions.

1) Non-interactive quick command (recommended):
```bash
npm create vite@latest my-app -- --template react-ts
cd my-app
npm install
npm run dev
```
This usually avoids extra prompts by specifying the template. If the command still asks questions, see step 2.

2) If the CLI prompts you interactively, here are the typical questions and the simplest answers:
- "Target directory 'my-app' is not empty. Proceed?":
	- If the folder contains important files, cancel (Ctrl+C) and pick a different name or move the folder.
	- If it's safe to replace, choose "Remove existing files and continue."
- "Which framework?": choose "React."
- "Which variant?": choose "react-ts" (TypeScript template).
- "Which linter to use?": choose "None" for the most basic setup (you can add ESLint later).
- "Which package manager?": choose "npm" unless you already use `yarn` or `pnpm`.

3) After creation:
```bash
cd my-app
npm install   # if the CLI didn't already install dependencies
npm run dev
```
Open the URL shown in the terminal (usually http://localhost:5173) to view the app.

4) If you prefer Create React App instead (slightly heavier but very stable):
```bash
npx create-react-app my-app --template typescript
cd my-app
npm start
```

Notes on directory issues:
- To avoid prompts, pick a project name that doesn't exist yet:
```bash
npm create vite@latest new-app -- --template react-ts
```
- To keep an existing folder and create a new one instead:
```bash
mv my-app my-app-backup
npm create vite@latest my-app -- --template react-ts
```



## 5 — If the command line feels scary (no-install options)
- Use an online editor to try React immediately: CodeSandbox (https://codesandbox.io) or StackBlitz (https://stackblitz.com).
- These run in your browser and need no local install.

## 6 — What to do next (suggested learning steps)
- Open the project in a code editor (VS Code is a popular choice).
- Edit `src/App.js` (or `src/App.jsx`) and save to see live updates.
- Learn about components, props, and state in small examples.

